--- body ---
## Context
CLAUDE.md states the rule plainly: "every measurement quoted in prose is
declared there with an anchor; when you quote a number from `bench/results/`,
declare it; when you re-measure, the guard tells you every prose site to
update." The guard is `bench/check_figures.py`, and it walks a declared map
of prose files.

`CONSTRAINTS.md` is not in that map. It is not in `PROSE` at all, so no
figure it carries is checked, and none can be — declaring one today would
fail on a missing key rather than protect anything.

That is a gap between a promise and a mechanism, and C2 is where it costs
most. C2 is the owning document for platform facts, and platform facts are
mostly numbers: after PR 33 it carries roughly a dozen, among them 670 680
distinct terms, 19 139 711 (term, document) pairs, 135 973 731 occurrences,
13 090 content documents, 13 631 cache files, 819,4 MiB, the 4 708 / 3 882
extractor-generation split, and 4 471 of 4 708 form-feed agreements. None of
them is guarded. Several were measured this session and will be re-measured.

The repo's own CLAUDE.md names the failure mode this invites: "Duplicated
numbers drift — this repo's most expensive recurring defect."

## What makes this more than bookkeeping
The guard's docstring argues, correctly, that it should not scan prose for
numbers and attribute them: that direction has no ground truth and produces
noise until someone turns it off. So the fix is not "make the guard clever
about CONSTRAINTS.md". It is to decide which of C2's figures are
load-bearing, give each an artifact and an anchor, and declare those.

Which means the real question this ticket has to answer first: **most of C2's
numbers have no `bench/results/` artifact at all.** They came from ad-hoc
probes of a live Zotero installation that no committed driver reproduces.
A figure with no artifact cannot be declared, so either the probes become
drivers that emit JSON, or those figures are knowingly left unguarded and
the promise in CLAUDE.md is narrowed to say so.

## Actions
1. Inventory the figures in CONSTRAINTS.md, C2 first, and mark each as
   backed by a `bench/results/` artifact, backed by a committed probe that
   could emit one, or unreproducible as written.
2. For the platform probes, decide whether `verification/probes/` scripts
   should emit JSON into `bench/results/` so their figures become
   declarable. `verification/probes/sdt_read.py` is the live example: it
   reproduces its numbers on demand but writes no artifact.
3. Add `CONSTRAINTS.md` to `PROSE` in `bench/check_figures.py` and declare
   the figures that have artifacts, with anchors, not presence-only.
4. If some figures stay unguarded by decision, say so in CLAUDE.md rather
   than leaving the general promise standing over an exception.

## Test
The guard's own suite (`tests/test_check_figures.py`) is the place. A
declared CONSTRAINTS.md figure whose artifact value is changed must fail the
guard; verify by sabotage, as the anchor mechanism itself was verified.

## Verification
- [ ] A deliberately stale CONSTRAINTS.md figure fails `make figures`
- [ ] The inventory names every C2 figure and its backing, including the
      ones that have none

## Invariants
- The guard keeps its declared-map design. No scanning prose for numbers.
- C2 stays the owning document for platform facts; this ticket changes how
  its numbers are checked, never who owns them.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] CONSTRAINTS.md is in the guard's map and its declarable figures are
      declared with anchors
- [ ] Every C2 figure without an artifact is either given one or recorded as
      knowingly unguarded
- [ ] `make check` green
